# Frontend environment — what a fresh clone needs to know
#
# There is no `.env` here. Next.js reads `.env.development` during `npm run dev`
# and `.env.production` during `npm run build`, and both are committed because
# they contain exactly one variable, which is public by construction.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ONLY VARIABLE: NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Anything prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_ is compiled into the JavaScript bundle and is
# readable by anyone who opens devtools. It is for a URL, never for a secret.
# The Gemini key lives in the backend only — see backend/.env.example.
#
#   npm run dev    (.env.development)  http://127.0.0.1:8008
#     The UI is on :3000 and the API on :8008, so the browser needs an absolute
#     URL. Change this if the backend runs elsewhere.
#
#   npm run build  (.env.production)   empty
#     Empty means "same origin". The exported UI is served by the FastAPI backend
#     itself, so API calls hit the same host and no CORS configuration is
#     involved. This is the single-process demo path.
#
# For a split deployment (UI on Vercel, API on a VPS) do NOT edit
# .env.production — set NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL in the hosting provider's environment
# settings. A value already present in the environment wins over the file, so the
# committed empty default stays correct for the single-process build.
#
# See DEPLOY.md for the full topology.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8008
